"# Appendix — The Verbs Cookbook

Entity-registry verbs — the small crew that manages the collection of live entities as a whole rather than one at a time. Every example verified against `bin/motoi eval`.

---

## Enumerate: `entity/all`

Returns the list of every live entity's id, in creation order. Nullary. Handy for iterating over the world when you need to do something to *every* entity — draw them all, tick them all, count them by kind.

```scheme
(entity/all)                                                     ; => ()
(begin (entity/make 'p1 10 20) (entity/all))                     ; => (\"p1\")
(begin (entity/make 'a 0 0) (entity/make 'b 10 10) (entity/all)) ; => (\"p1\" \"a\" \"b\")
```

Look closely at the third line: `\"p1\"` is still there. There is one entity registry for the whole session, and these three examples run one after another, so the `p1` made on the line above has not gone anywhere. `entity/all` reports the *world*, not the entities made by the expression you just typed. If you want a clean slate, remove what you made.

When you call `entity/make` you supply the id yourself as a symbol, and it is preserved as a string — that is why these read back as `\"p1\"`, `\"a\"`, `\"b\"`. `entity/spawn` is the other constructor: it generates the id for you, and the ids it generates are **numbers** counting up from 1, not strings. So a world built with both constructors gives you a mixed list, and code that assumes every id is a string will be wrong half the time. Use `entity/id` to get an entity's id rather than guessing its spelling.

---

## Scratch storage: `entity/ref`

Every entity carries a *scratch map* — a per-entity key/value store for whatever data you want to attach without extending the physics record. `entity/ref id key` reads a value; `entity/set!` (already in the base chapters) writes one. Missing keys return the special symbol `'nan` so a null return does not collide with a legitimate `#f` value.

```scheme
(begin (entity/make 'p1 10 20)
       (entity/set! 'p1 'hp 3)
       (entity/ref 'p1 'hp))
; => 3

(begin (entity/make 'p1 10 20)
       (entity/ref 'p1 'nothing))
; => nan
```

Use scratch for anything the built-in physics does not model — hit points, ammo counts, dialogue state, the entity's favourite colour. Anything a two-line game needs but the shared entity record should not.

---

## Removal: `entity/remove!`

Deletes an entity from the registry. Returns `#t` if the entity existed and got removed, `#f` if the id was already gone or never valid. `entity/remove!` is a *destructive* alias of `entity/despawn!` — same effect, shorter name for people who prefer the register/remove pairing.

```scheme
(begin (entity/make 'p1 10 20)
       (entity/remove! 'p1)
       (entity/all))
; => (\"a\" \"b\")

(entity/remove! 'nonexistent)          ; => #f
```

`p1` is gone, and `a` and `b` are the entities left over from the enumerate section above — the same one shared registry. Removing an entity removes exactly that entity; it is not a reset.

After removal the id is free — a future `entity/make 'p1 ...` would create a fresh entity under the same name, with no state carried over from the removed one.

---

## Tag removal: `entity/untag!`

`entity/untag! id tag` removes an entity from a named group. Entities created via `entity/spawn` are auto-tagged with their kind, so you can un-tag them by kind name.

Call it for the effect, not for an answer: the reference gives its result as **void**. Do not test what it hands back and do not branch on it. In particular it does not report whether the tag was there — untagging a group the entity was never in is simply a no-op, and so is untagging an id that does not exist at all. Neither raises.

```scheme
(define hero (entity/spawn 'hero 5 5))
(entity/untag! (entity/id hero) 'hero)
(entity/alive? (entity/id hero))
; => #t

(entity/untag! \"never-existed\" 'anything)
(entity/alive? \"never-existed\")
; => #f
```

The first example is the point worth remembering: untagging changes the entity's group membership and nothing else. The hero is still alive, still at `(5, 5)`, still a `hero` by kind — it has only left the group named `hero`. Kind is what an entity *is*; a tag is a group you chose to put it in, and only the second one is what `entity/untag!` touches.

Note that `entity/spawn` takes a position — `kind x y` — and hands back the entity itself, not its id. Ask for the id with `entity/id` rather than assuming a spelling.

Use it when a stateful entity outgrows its group — the pickup that became a projectile, the enemy that turned friendly.

---

## Closing note

Every example in this appendix was checked against `bin/motoi eval` on 2026-07-17. Entity ids are strings once they land in the registry, regardless of whether they came in as symbols.

---

## Game-theory extras (wired 2026-07-22)

The `game/*` game-theory kit lands 21 verbs at the prompt: `game/nim-sum`, `game/nim-outcome`, `game/mex`, `game/grundy`, `game/star-n`, `game/wythoff-p?`, and the surreal-number family (`game/surreal`, `game/surreal-lit`, `game/surreal-simplest`, `game/surreal-eq?`, `game/surreal-le?`, `game/surreal-is-number?`, `game/surreal-birthday`, `game/surreal-neg`, `game/surreal-add`, `game/surreal-sub`, `game/surreal-mul`, `game/to-real`, `game/temperature`) plus tic-tac-toe (`game/ttt-value`, `game/ttt-best-move`). Use them to build cabinet-worthy Nim tables, tic-tac-toe teachers, and Wythoff-style pebble games.

```scheme
(game/nim-sum 1 2 3)                                ; => 0   ; P-position
(game/nim-outcome (list 1 2 3))                     ; => P
(game/mex (list 0 1 3))                             ; => 2
(game/wythoff-p? 3 5)                               ; => #t
(game/ttt-value (list 'x 'o '- '- 'x '- '- '- 'o))  ; => x-win
(game/ttt-best-move (list '- '- '- '- 'x '- '- '- '-))  ; => 0
```

Every game-theory verb is pure and eager. Import nothing, call anywhere.
")
